>
  <h2>What&rsquo;s the difference between a correction, a corrective action and preventive action?</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>nonconformance</strong> is anything that doesn&rsquo;t meet a requirement &mdash; a wrong label, an out-of-spec
    result, a missed step in a process. A <strong>correction</strong> deals with that specific instance: you rework the batch,
    replace the carton, redo the inspection. A <strong>corrective action</strong> goes further and addresses why it happened, so
    the same nonconformance doesn&rsquo;t turn up again next month. Doing the first without the second is the single most common
    way a CAPA (corrective and preventive action) process falls short.
  </p>
  <p>
    Take a mislabelled carton as an example. Pulling the wrong cartons off the line and relabelling them correctly is the
    correction &mdash; it fixes today&rsquo;s problem. Working out that the label rolls for two similar products were stored next
    to each other, and moving them apart or adding a barcode check at the labelling station, is the corrective action &mdash; it
    stops the same mix-up happening again next week.
  </p>
  <p>
    Preventive action, as its own separate clause, no longer exists in ISO 9001:2015, and ISO 9001:2026, the sixth edition
    published in September 2026, doesn&rsquo;t bring it back. Both editions replace it with risk-based thinking woven into
    planning throughout the QMS &mdash; you&rsquo;re expected to think about risks and opportunities before something goes
    wrong, rather than run a bolt-on "preventive action" process after the fact. ISO 9001:2026 goes a step further and splits
    risk and opportunity into separate parts of clause 6.1, so opportunity-spotting gets its own explicit attention rather than
    riding along with risk. In practice, many small teams still find it useful to ask, once a corrective action is in place,
    whether the same root cause could show up anywhere else in the business &mdash; that forward-looking check is where
    preventive thinking still earns its place, even without a clause named after it.
  </p>

  <h2>What does ISO 9001 clause 10.2 actually require?</h2>
  <p>
    Clause 10.2, nonconformity and corrective action, sets out what to do when something doesn&rsquo;t meet requirements,
    including a complaint. React to the nonconformity: correct it, and deal with the consequences. Then evaluate whether
    corrective action is needed to stop it happening again &mdash; reviewing and analysing the nonconformity, working out its
    cause, and checking whether similar nonconformities exist or could occur elsewhere. If action is needed, implement it, review
    how effective it was, and update risks and the QMS itself if the nonconformity revealed something you&rsquo;d missed. Finally,
    keep documented information as evidence of the nonconformity, the actions taken, and the results. This requirement is
    substantively the same in ISO 9001:2026: clause 10 is reorganised so continual improvement, previously its own clause 10.3,
    merges into clause 10.1, but clause 10.2 itself keeps its number and its content.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two things in that list are easy to skip under time pressure: deciding whether the same cause could turn up somewhere else,
    and reviewing whether the action actually worked. Both are explicit requirements, not optional extras.
  </p>
  <p>
    The documented information clause 10.2 asks for is exactly what an auditor samples during a corrective action review: the
    original finding, what was done immediately, what the investigation found, what action was taken and who approved it, and
    the result of checking it later. A record that&rsquo;s missing any one of those pieces reads, to an auditor, as a step that
    was skipped rather than one that just wasn&rsquo;t written down &mdash; the same discipline our <a
    href="/guides/iso-9001-document-control">guide to document control requirements</a> covers for clause 7.5 applies here too.
  </p>

  <h2>What&rsquo;s a lean six-step process for closing a nonconformance?</h2>
  <p>
    You don&rsquo;t need a heavyweight system to satisfy clause 10.2 &mdash; you need a process that reliably covers each of its
    steps, in order, every time. A workable version looks like this:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Report &amp; record</strong> &mdash; anyone raises the nonconformance, with enough detail to describe what happened and where.</li>
    <li><strong>Immediate correction</strong> &mdash; the specific problem is contained: stock quarantined, a batch held, a wrong document withdrawn.</li>
    <li><strong>Investigation &amp; severity assessment</strong> &mdash; someone competent looks into what happened and rates how serious it is, which decides how much scrutiny the rest of the process gets.</li>
    <li><strong>Root cause analysis</strong> &mdash; the real cause is identified, not just the immediate trigger.</li>
    <li><strong>Corrective &amp; preventive action</strong> &mdash; an action is agreed and implemented to address the root cause, and approved by someone with the authority to sign it off.</li>
    <li><strong>Verification</strong> &mdash; after enough time has passed for the action to prove itself, someone checks the evidence and confirms whether it worked.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The record only closes at the end of step six, not when the corrective action is first put in place. A nonconformance
    marked "closed" the same day the fix went live hasn&rsquo;t actually been verified &mdash; it&rsquo;s been assumed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Not every nonconformance needs the same weight behind it. A minor labelling slip caught before it left the building might
    move through all six steps in a day, with a light-touch investigation and a short verification window. A nonconformance
    that reached a customer, or points to a systemic issue such as a supplier consistently missing spec, deserves a proper
    investigation, a named approver, and a longer verification period before you&rsquo;re confident it&rsquo;s actually fixed.
    Rating severity at step three is what lets you make that call consistently, instead of case by case.
  </p>

  <h2>Which root-cause methods should you use?</h2>
  <p>
    You don&rsquo;t need a different method for every nonconformance &mdash; two simple techniques cover most small-company
    cases, and you can reach for something heavier, like an 8D report or an FMEA, when a nonconformance is genuinely complex or
    high-risk.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>5 Whys</strong> &mdash; ask "why" repeatedly, starting from the nonconformance itself, until you reach a cause you
      can actually act on. "The carton was mislabelled" leads to "why" the operator picked the wrong label, "why" the labels
      weren&rsquo;t clearly separated at the workstation, and so on, until the answer is a system issue rather than a person.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fishbone (Ishikawa) diagram</strong> &mdash; useful when a nonconformance could have more than one contributing
      cause. You sort possible causes into categories such as people, process, equipment and materials, which helps a group
      spot a cause that a single "why" chain might miss.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How do you verify a corrective action worked, and close the nonconformance?</h2>
  <p>
    Set a verification date that gives the action time to prove itself &mdash; immediately checking whether a retrained
    operator makes the same mistake again tells you nothing useful; checking a month or a quarter later does. When that date
    arrives, look at real evidence: has the same nonconformance, or the same root cause, occurred again anywhere it could have?
    If it hasn&rsquo;t, mark the action effective and close the record. If it has, mark it ineffective, and go back to root
    cause analysis rather than closing the record anyway.
  </p>
  <p>
    This step is what separates a CAPA process that actually reduces repeat problems from one that just generates paperwork.
    Skipping it, or treating it as a formality, is the fastest way to end up fixing the same nonconformance every quarter.
  </p>
  <p>
    In the mislabelled-carton example above, verification isn&rsquo;t "did we move the label rolls" &mdash; that&rsquo;s just
    confirming the action happened. It&rsquo;s checking, a month or a quarter later, that no mislabelled carton has turned up
    since, which tells you the actual root cause was addressed rather than just one visible symptom of it.
  </p>

